\begin_layout Subsubsection
Specifying a set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Extensionality implies that to specify a set,
 it suffices either to list its elements or to provide a property that characterize the latter among the elements of a possibly larger set.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Roster notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition

\emph on
Roster
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
enumeration notation
\emph default
 specifies a set by listing its elements between braces,
 separated by commas.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example
The notations 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\}$
\end_inset

 for the empty set and 
\begin_inset Formula $\{x\}$
\end_inset

 for a singleton are examples of roster notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When specifying a set,
 all that matters is whether each potential element is in the set or not,
 so a set does not change if elements are repeated or arranged in a different order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When there is a clear pattern for generating all set elements,
 one can use an ellipsis to abbreviate the notation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Set–builder notation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Set–builder notation specifies a set as being the set of all elements that satisfy some logical formula.
 More precisely,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\varpi(x)$
\end_inset

 is a logical formula depending on a variable 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 which evalutates to 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\bot$
\end_inset

 depending on the value of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\{x\mid\varpi(x)\}
\]

\end_inset

denotes the set of all 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 for which 
\begin_inset Formula $\varpi(x)$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

.
 In this notation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mid$
\end_inset

 is read as 
\emph on
such that
\emph default
.
\end_layout

