\begin_layout Subsection
Set relations and operations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two types of things one can do with sets:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One can compare sets and see their relationship.
 These are called 
\emph on
set relations
\emph default
.
 Equality is the first such relation encountered this far.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
One can produce new sets from other sets.
 These are called 
\emph on
set operations
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Subsets
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As seen before,
 sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 are equal iff they have the same elements,
 denoted as 
\begin_inset Formula $A=B$
\end_inset

.
 Equality is reflexive;
 for every set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B=B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
subset
\emph default
 of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 such that every element of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is also an element of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\subseteq B\Longleftrightarrow\forall x\mid x:A\Longrightarrow x:B.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relationship between sets established by 
\begin_inset Formula $\subseteq$
\end_inset

 is called inclusion or containment.
 Containment is reflexive too;
 every set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a subset of itself,
 namely 
\begin_inset Formula $B\subseteq B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is a 
\emph on
proper subset
\emph default
 of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subseteq B$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A\neq B$
\end_inset

;
 to denote this one writes 
\begin_inset Formula $A\subset B$
\end_inset

,
 therefore
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A\subset B\Longleftrightarrow A\subseteq B\wedge A\neq B.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Properties of containment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Containment is antisymmetric;
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall A,B\mid A=B\Longleftrightarrow A\subseteq B\wedge A\supseteq B.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The empty set is a subset of every set:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing\subseteq A$
\end_inset

.
 In particular,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A\neq\varnothing$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing\subset A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Containment is also transitive;
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall A,B,C\mid A\subseteq B\wedge B\subseteq C\Longrightarrow A\subseteq C.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

