\begin_layout Subsection
Functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
function
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 from a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to a set 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is a rule that assigns to each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 a unique element of 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The notation 
\begin_inset Formula $f:A\to B$
\end_inset

 denotes a function 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.The result of applying 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 to an element 
\begin_inset Formula $x:A$
\end_inset

 is denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)$
\end_inset

;
 it is called the 
\emph on
value
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

,
 or the 
\emph on
image
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 under 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
 The set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\emph on
domain
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 is called the 
\emph on
codomain
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

.
 The set 
\begin_inset Formula $f(A)\subseteq B$
\end_inset

