\begin_layout Minisec
Indexed family
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Intuitively,
 an indexed family is a collection whose elements are labelled with the elements of an arbitrary (index) set 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

.
 These labels allow the same element to occur several times in the family.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
An indexed family is a function that has an index set 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 as its domain.
 Generally,
 the usual functional notation is not used for indexed families.
 Instead,
 the element 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 of the index set 
\begin_inset Formula $I$
\end_inset

 is written as a subscript of the same name of the family.
 Special cases include:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\mathbb{B}=\{\bot,\top\}$
\end_inset

 defines an ordered pair,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\mathbb{N}_{n}=\{0,...,n-1\}$
\end_inset

 (
\begin_inset Formula $\forall n:\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

) defines an 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

–tuple,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Formula $I=\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 defines a sequence.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Family of sets operations become indexed operations:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\bigcup\nolimits_{i:I}A_{i}=\{x\mid\exists i:I\mid x:A_{i}\}\text{ and }\bigcap\nolimits_{i:I}A_{i}=\{x\mid\forall i:I\mid x:A_{i}\}.
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

For intersection,
 
\begin_inset Formula $I\neq\varnothing$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

