\begin_layout Minisec
Set exponentiation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Given sets 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

,
 the 
\emph on
set exponentiation
\emph default
,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{A}$
\end_inset

,
 is the set that has as elements all functions 
\begin_inset Formula $A\rightarrow B$
\end_inset

,
 i.e.:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B^{A}=\{f:A\rightarrow B\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Equivalently,
 
\begin_inset Formula $B^{A}$
\end_inset

 can be viewed as the Cartesian product of a family,
 indexed by 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 of sets that are all equal to 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 This explains the terminology and the notation,
 since exponentiation with integer exponents is a product where all factors are equal to the base:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
B^{A}=\prod\nolimits_{a:A}B=\{(b_{a})_{a:A}\mid\forall a:A\mid b_{a}:B\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Power set
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The power set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{P}(A)$
\end_inset

 of a set 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is the set of of all subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

,
 which include 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a natural bijection between the subsets 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 and their indicator functions 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathds{1}_{B}:A\to\mathbb{B}$
\end_inset

,
 by which
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathcal{P}(A)=\mathbb{B}^{A}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In particular,
 given 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert\mathbb{B}|=2$
\end_inset

,
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 is finite,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\vert\mathbb{B}^{A}\vert=2^{\vert A\vert}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Relations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In mathematics,
 a binary relation denotes some kind of relationship between two objects in a set,
 which may or may not hold.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Given a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 a binary relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcirc$
\end_inset

 over 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a set of ordered pairs of elements from 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 formally 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcirc\subseteq X\times X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The statement 
\begin_inset Formula $(a,b):\bigcirc$
\end_inset

 reads in infix notation as 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bigcirc b$
\end_inset

.
 The order of the elements is important;
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $a\neq b$
\end_inset

 the 
\begin_inset Formula $b\bigcirc a$
\end_inset

 can be 
\begin_inset Formula $\top$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\bot$
\end_inset

 independently of 
\begin_inset Formula $a\bigcirc b$
\end_inset

.
 The complement of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcirc$
\end_inset

 with the universal set 
\begin_inset Formula $X\times X$
\end_inset

 is also a binary relation,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\cancel{\bigcirc}$
\end_inset

,
 defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall a,b:X\mid a\mathrel{\cancel{\bigcirc}}b=\neg(a\bigcirc b).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Naturally 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcirc\cap\cancel{\bigcirc}=\varnothing$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\bigcirc\cup\cancel{\bigcirc}=X\times X$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Being a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $X\times X$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\varnothing$
\end_inset

 is equivalently identified by its corresponding indicator function 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathds{1}_{\bigcirc}:X\times X\to\mathbb{B}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

