\begin_layout Subsubsection
Equivalence relation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
equivalence relation
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathord{\sim}$
\end_inset

 is a binary relation on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 that is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
reflexive 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a:X\mid a\sim a,$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
symmetric 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b:X\mid a\sim b\Longleftrightarrow b\sim a,$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
transitive 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b,c:X\mid a\sim b\wedge b\sim c\Longrightarrow a\sim c.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Definition
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 together with the relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 is called a 
\emph on
setoid
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\nsim$
\end_inset

 on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 an equivalence relation (unless 
\begin_inset Formula $X=\varnothing$
\end_inset

),
 as,
 contrary to an equivalence relation,
 it is:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
irreflexive 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a:X\mid\neg(a\nsim a),$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Related important definitions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
Given an equivalence relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a:X$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
[a]=\{x:X\mid x\sim a\}\subseteq X
\]

\end_inset

is the equivalence class to which 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 belongs and is a representative of.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 equivalent to each other are also elements of the same equivalence class.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall b,c:X\mid(b\sim c\wedge[b]=[c])\vee(b\nsim c\wedge[b]\cap[c]=\varnothing)
\]

\end_inset

Therefore element of 
\begin_inset Formula $[a]$
\end_inset

 can be a representative of 
\begin_inset Formula $[a]$
\end_inset

,
 or more strongly,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall a,b:X\mid a\sim b\Longleftrightarrow[a]=[b].
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
partition
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is a set 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 of nonempty subsets of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 such that every element of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 is an element of a single element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

.
 Each element of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 is a cell of the partition.
 Moreover:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the elements of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

 are pairwise disjoint,
 or 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall A,B:\mathcal{S}\mid A\neq B\Longrightarrow A\cap B=\varnothing,$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
and their union is 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

,
 or
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bigcup\mathcal{S}=X.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The fundamental theorem of equivalence relations state that an equivalence relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 partitions 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 by its equivalence classes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
The set of all equivalence classes of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 by an equivalence relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

,
 denoted 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
X/\mathord{\sim}\coloneqq\{[x]\mid x:X\},
\]

\end_inset

is the 
\emph on
quotient set
\emph default
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
According to the fundamental theorem,
 
\begin_inset Formula $X/\mathord{\sim}$
\end_inset

 is the partition of 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 generated by 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 induces 
\begin_inset Formula $=$
\end_inset

 (equality) on 
\begin_inset Formula $X/\mathord{\sim}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Remark
Equality 
\begin_inset Formula $=$
\end_inset

 is a trivial equivalence relation that partitions a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 to its elements (as singletons),
 as,
 by efinition,
 every element in a set equals no other element in it.
 For a family of sets 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathcal{S}$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $=$
\end_inset

 is an equivalence relation.
\end_layout

