\begin_layout Subsubsection
Order
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In mathematics,
 a partial order on a set is an arrangement such that,
 for certain pairs of elements,
 one precedes the other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Partial
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The word partial is used to indicate that not every pair of elements needs to be comparable;
 that is,
 there may be pairs for which neither element precedes the other.
 Partial orders thus generalize total orders,
 in which every pair is comparable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
partial order
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathord{\leq}$
\end_inset

 is a binary relation on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 that is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
reflexive 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a:X\mid a\leq a,$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
antisymmetric 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b:X\mid a\leq b\wedge b\leq a\Longrightarrow a=b,$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
transitive 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b,c:X\mid a\leq b\wedge b\leq c\Longrightarrow a\leq c.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
A 
\emph on
partially ordered set
\emph default
 (
\emph on
poset
\emph default
 for short) is a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 (called the ground set of the order) equipped with a partial order.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 be a set of representatives equipped with an equivalence relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

.
 A reflexive,
 transitive relation 
\begin_inset Formula $\lesssim$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 induces a partial order 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $X=R/\mathord{\sim}$
\end_inset

 provided
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall a,b:R\mid a\sim b\Longleftrightarrow a\lesssim b\wedge b\lesssim a.
\]

\end_inset

The quotient order is defined by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall a,b:R\mid[a]\leq[b]\Longleftrightarrow a\lesssim b.
\]

\end_inset

This order is independent of representatives.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 is equality on 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

,
 the quotient identifies with 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 itself.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We use one partial–order interface on representatives,
 with equivalence as equality of the represented values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
strict partial order
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $<$
\end_inset

 is a binary relation on 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 that is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
irreflexive 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a:X\mid a\nless a,$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Description
transitive 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b,c:X\mid a<b\wedge b<c\Longrightarrow a<c.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
For the quotient order above,
 strict comparison is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall a,b:R\mid[a]<[b]\Longleftrightarrow a\lesssim b\wedge a\nsim b.
\]

\end_inset

On representatives,
 we use the same comparison symbols,
 with equivalence in place of value equality.
\end_layout

