\begin_layout Standard
The weak comparisons form a converse cycle.
 Implementing either one grounds the remaining defaults:
 equality is mutual weak comparison,
 and the strict comparisons exclude equivalent operands.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Minisec
Total
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
total order
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq$
\end_inset

 is a partial order on some set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 in which any two elements are comparable,
 or that is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
strongly
\begin_inset space ~
\end_inset

connected 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b:X\mid a\leq b\vee b\leq a.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Definition
Note that,
 reflexivity in total orders follows from strong connectivity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition
A 
\emph on
strict total order
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $<$
\end_inset

 is a strict partial order on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 in which any two distinct elements are comparable,
 or that is:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Description
connected 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a,b:X\mid a\neq b\Longrightarrow a<b\vee b<a.$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
A total order 
\begin_inset Formula $\leq$
\end_inset

 on a set 
\begin_inset Formula $X$
\end_inset

 implies a strict total order 
\begin_inset Formula $<$
\end_inset

 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall a,b:X\mid a<b\Longleftrightarrow a\leq b\wedge a\neq b\Longleftrightarrow\neg(b\leq a).
\]

\end_inset

The negated form relies on totality;
 it is not valid for incomparable classes in a partial order.
\end_layout

