.
 Its carrier is motivated by the quotient constructions of the integers and rational numbers,
 beginning with the natural numbers
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{N}=\{0,1,2,\ldots\}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The set of integers 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Z}$
\end_inset

 can be constructed from pairs of natural numbers,
 interpreted as formal differences.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathord{\sim}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}$
\end_inset

 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall(a,b),(a^{\prime},b^{\prime}):\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}\mid(a,b)\sim(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})\Longleftrightarrow a+b^{\prime}=a^{\prime}+b.
\]

\end_inset

Equivalent pairs represent the same integer.
 Each class has a unique representative with at least one zero component,
 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{Z}=(\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N})/\mathord{\sim}.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Similarly,
 let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Z}^{*}$
\end_inset

 be the non–zero integers.
 Rational numbers are represented by pairs in 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 Define 
\begin_inset Formula $\sim$
\end_inset

 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall(a,b),(a^{\prime},b^{\prime}):\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}^{*}\mid(a,b)\sim(a^{\prime},b^{\prime})\Longleftrightarrow ab^{\prime}=a^{\prime}b,
\]

\end_inset

so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{Q}=(\mathbb{Z}\times\mathbb{Z}^{*})/\mathord{\sim}.
\]

\end_inset

The non–zero second component ensures that each pair represents a defined rational number.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For the numeric carrier considered here,
 write 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{N}^{*}=\mathbb{N}\setminus\{0\}$
\end_inset

.
 The positive rational numbers have the representation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{Q}^{+}=(\mathbb{N}^{*}\times\mathbb{N}^{*})/\mathord{\sim}.
\]

\end_inset

Allowing a zero numerator gives the non–negative rationals 
\begin_inset Formula $(\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N}^{*})/\mathord{\sim}$
\end_inset

.
 Allowing a zero denominator adjoins a single class denoted 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset

,
 represented by 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall a\in\mathbb{N}\mid(a,0)$
\end_inset

.
 Applying the same cross–product relation to non–zero pairs gives the extended non–negative rational numbers:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{\mathbb{Q}}^{+}=(\mathbb{N}\times\mathbb{N})^{*}/\mathord{\sim}.
\]

\end_inset

The pair 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,0)$
\end_inset

 is excluded because the cross–product equivalence would relate it to every pair and fail transitivity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
As a separate input convention,
 a constructor may replace 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,0)$
\end_inset

 by 
\begin_inset Formula $(1,1)$
\end_inset

 before constructing a value.
 The input pair is not itself a representative in the quotient;
 this convention does not alter fraction equivalence.
 This quotient is the carrier of the numeric type to be developed in 
